# Allowlist for `resq secrets`. One pattern per line, `#` starts a comment.
# A finding is suppressed when its file path or its content contains a pattern
# (secrets.rs:701). Read from the repo root by default (secrets.rs:804);
# override with --allowlist.
#
# CONTENT patterns only — deliberately no file paths. A path entry silences a
# whole file forever, including a real credential added to it later. Each
# pattern below names one specific piece of known-synthetic text, so every other
# line of every file stays scanned.
#
# Note when adding entries: findings are matched against `redact_line(line)`,
# not the raw line, so only the first ~10 and last ~6 characters of a long line
# are visible to a pattern. Keep patterns short and anchored at the start.

# --- crates/resq-cli/src/commands/secrets.rs -------------------------------
#
# The scanner's own rule table stores the PEM armour headers it searches for as
# Rust raw-string literals, so seven of its private-key rules match their own
# definitions. The `r"` prefix is what makes this narrow: it only appears where
# a pattern is being DECLARED. A leaked key is PEM text, not a Rust raw-string
# literal, so real keys anywhere in this repo are still reported.
r"-----BEG

# Synthetic fixtures in that file's test module, used to prove the AWS and
# GitHub rules fire. Both are obviously fake — a real AWS key id is AKIA plus 16
# random characters, not a counting sequence. Matched on their exact prefixes so
# a genuine key of either shape elsewhere is unaffected.
AKIA123456
ghp_abcdef
